  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for heavy rain/localized flooding this\nafternoon into tonight \n\n12hr rainfall through noon EDT was generally 0.50-1.50 falling to\nthe west of US-15. This has lead to some pre-saturation which\ncould play a factor if additional heavy rain develops over these\nareas. \n\nExtensive cloud cover and time of day contributed to higher \nstability and effectively capped max rainfall rates below 1\"/hr.\nThe environment has been undergoing some modest destablization \ninto the afternoon with thermodynamic mesoanalysis depicting \nSB/MLCAPE values on the rise with plenty of moisture available \n(1.5-2.2\" PWAT). The destabilizing boundary layer interacting \nwith a positively titled shortwave trough extending from Lower \nMI southwestward into the Mid-South will result in expanding \ncoverage of heavy showers/t-storms with more intense rain rates \nwhich should lead to increasing flash flood potential through \nthe rest of the afternoon into the evening hours. Breaks in the \nclouds particularly entering the western part of the CWA could \nalso spell an uptick in t-storm intensity and potential for \nisolated strong to severe storms. WPC and SPC made very little\nto no changes to the 16/1630Z updates to the ERO and SWO for \nthe rest of today. Cluster of storms with heavy rain working\nnorthward from DC/northern MD into the Lower Susquehanna Valley\nalso is worth watching for more of an urban flash flood threat\n(given less compromised FFG) and low-topped spins. Expect heavy\nshowers and storms to slowly progress eastward through tonight\nand can't rule out some localized runoff issues outside the\nwatch area farther to the east through the Susquehanna Valley. \n\nFor now, the best heavy rain/severe wx signal is to the south \nand east of CPA tomorrow/Monday. Heading into the work week, \nlatest model QPF data now favors a possible break in the rain \nthrough early week before daily shower/t-storm chances return \nWed-Fri. Some localized heavy rain and marginal flooding risk \nwill continue into late week.",
